  "notes": "Magic Johnson Music soloist built on Steve \"Stone\" Huff's bass-first ballad grammar, 2000–2006. The research gives \"58–84 half-time (backbeat on 3) plus one straight outlier at 108\" and eleven sourced tempos inside the felt band, so 58–84 with `halfTime` true is the base. The drum deltas are the producer's own quote: Huff builds \"very minimal but thumping from the bass\" — \"the kit is a single kick per bar, a clap/snare on beat 3, and nothing else; percussion appears as a ground-stomp or hand-clap layer rather than hats\". So the kick is one hit with syncopation 0.03, the hat lane is an 8th base at 0.01 fill density with the open hat off, and the percussion is a snap plus a low tom at −5 dB placed on downbeats, which is the nearest the lane vocabulary comes to a stomp. The identifying parameter is the bass, and the research states it as method: Huff approaches every record \"from a bass player's standpoint\" and \"the bassline is the lead instrument, a legato fingered line with slides and passing tones, mixed above the drums\". So `followRootsProb` is 0.34 — the lowest in this section — `glideProb` is 0.42, the passing-tone set is the widest anywhere here, `anticipationProb` is 0.5 and the cell shapes walk. \"Bass-implied harmony with a thin Rhodes/pad on top\" and \"live Chicago Symphony Orchestra strings\" are the modest extension spread and a `sustain_pad`-led counter at +12. ⚠ The research marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]`. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode with major-7th colour [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
